Consider a protoplanetary (transition) disc of gas and dust orbiting a young pre-main-sequence star, in which one or more giant planets have opened an annular gap. The outer disc has a pressure maximum at the outer edge of the gap that traps millimetre-sized solids ("dust filtration"), producing a millimetre-bright ring with a resolved cavity at sub-/millimetre wavelengths. The system is also observed to:
(i) accrete gas onto the central star at rates comparable to those of full primordial discs (10^-9 to 10^-8 solar masses per year), so gas continues to flow through the gap;
(ii) show little or no near-infrared excess in its spectral energy distribution, indicating a strong depletion of warm small (1–10 micron) dust grains in the inner disc inside the cavity;
(iii) display scattered-light and thermal-infrared images (e.g., polarimetric near-infrared and mid-infrared) showing structure consistent with a depleted but not entirely empty cavity for small grains.

Aerodynamic theory shows that grains in the size range roughly 1–10 microns are well coupled to the gas: their Stokes number is small enough that they are dragged with the accretion flow through the gap and are not retained at the outer pressure bump by dust filtration alone. Hence, if dust filtration were the only process at work, micron-sized grains would populate the inner disc, dominate the opacity, and produce a strong near-infrared excess inconsistent with observation.

Problem. Identify and quantitatively justify a physical mechanism (or combination of mechanisms) that, in the presence of one or more gap-opening giant planets, can simultaneously:
(a) remove or sufficiently trap 1–10 micron dust grains from the inner disc to suppress the near-infrared excess;
(b) preserve a millimetre-bright outer ring with a sharply defined cavity at millimetre wavelengths;
(c) allow gas to continue accreting onto the star at observed rates;
(d) reproduce the structure seen in scattered-light and thermal-infrared imaging of transition discs.

State the mechanism, derive the governing equations describing the coupled gas–dust dynamics in the gap region (continuity, radial drift with drag, and any additional forces such as radiation pressure or photoevaporative wind), and demonstrate consistency with all four observational constraints above.

